<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.7.3: Sorting Systems of Equations Based on Number of Solutions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00481</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.7.3: Sorting Systems of Equations Based on Number of Solutions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a63b9950-467a-4851-868e-59795673d248</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>

      <!-- BEGIN CLICK TO REVEAL NO TEXTBOX -->

      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Your teacher will give you a <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d3d4de9dfa1b7841334a3f1df2f3de034f188b27">set
            of cards</link>. Each card contains a system of equations.</para>
            <para id="para-00002">Sort the systems into three
            groups based on the number of solutions each system has. Be prepared to explain how you
            know where each system belongs.</para>

          <para id="para-00003">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>

          <list id="list-00001">
            <item>No solution: Cards 2 and 5</item>
            <item>One solution: Cards 1, 4, 6, and 9</item>
            <item>Infinitely many solutions: Cards 3, 7, and 8</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->

      <!-- END CLICK TO REVEAL NO TEXTBOX -->
<section id="sect-00002">
  <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
  <section class="practice" id="sect-00003"><title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      <!--Ready
      for More Question1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">In the cards, for each system with no solution, change a single constant term so
            that there are infinitely many solutions to the system.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>

          <para id="para-00007"> Card 2: change 3 to 13; Card 5: change <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> to 12.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Ready
      for More Question2-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">For each system with infinitely many solutions, change a single constant term so
            that there are no solutions to the system.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>

          <para id="para-00010"> Any change to a constant term would work.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Ready
      for More Question3-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00011">Explain why in these situations it is impossible to change a single constant term so
            that there is exactly one solution to the system.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>

          <para id="para-00013"> Changing a constant term won’t change the slope of the lines, so the graphs will be
            either distinct parallel lines or the same line.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </section>

    <section id="sect-00004">
      <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00014"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_2_7_3_SC"/></para>
          </problem>
          </exercise>
        </note>
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00005">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00006">
        <title>Classifying and Rewriting Systems of Equations</title>
        <para id="para-00016">Find a solution to the system of equations using the addition method.</para>
        <para id="para-00017"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00021"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00018"><emphasis>Solution </emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00019">With the addition method, we want to eliminate one of the variables by adding the
          equations. In this case, let’s focus on eliminating <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. If we multiply both sides of
          the first equation by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, then we will be able to eliminate the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-variable.</para>
        <para id="para-00020">Now add the equations:</para>
        <para id="para-00022"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00025"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00023">We can see that there will be an infinite number of situations that satisfy both
          equations.</para>

        <note class="try look-at-me" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Classifying and Rewriting Systems of Equations</title>
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00024">Change a single constant term in the system from above so that there are no
              solutions.</para>

            <para id="para-00030"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00026">Show that you were successful in changing the system to have no solution.</para>
            
            <para id="para-00027">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00028">Compare your answer: Here is how to rewrite the system so there is no solution.</para>

            <para id="para-00029">We can simply change the 2 in the first equation to any other number, such as 5.</para>

            <para id="para-00032"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00031">Let’s try to prove there is no solution. Using substitution:</para>

            <para id="para-00034"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00033">Substitute this expression into the second equation.</para>

            <para id="para-00036"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>15</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>≠</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00035">Since the equation is not true, the system has no solution.</para>

          </solution>
        </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>